## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical online order-statistics maintenance with batched updates and coordinate compression.

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree on value domain
- Coordinate compression
- k-th order statistic via prefix sums
- Batch amortization

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
class BIT {
  constructor(size) {
    this.tree = new Array(size + 1).fill(0);
  }

  update(idx, delta) {
    while (idx <= this.size) {
      this.tree[idx] += delta;
      idx += idx & -idx;
    }
  }

  query(k) {
    // Binary lift for k-th smallest
    let idx = 0;
    for (let i = Math.pow(2, Math.floor(Math.log2(this.size))); i > 0; i >>= 1) {
      if (idx + i <= this.size && this.tree[idx + i] < k) {
        k -= this.tree[idx + i];
        idx += i;
      }
    }
    return idx + 1;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does this adapt if k grows with n?
- What if updates can be deletions or duplicates?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory using probabilistic data structures; beginner-friendly but shows real trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for distinct count estimation
- Hashing, leading-zero counting, registers
- Time: O(1) per item, Memory: O(m)
- Error ~1.04/√m; choose m from m ≈ (1.04/ε)^2

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal HyperLogLog sketch (illustrative)
class HLL {
  constructor(p = 12) {
    this.m = 1 << p;
    this.reg = new Uint8Array(this.m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const h = hash64(item); // 64-bit int hash
    const p = Math.log2(this.m);
    const j = Number(h >>> (64 - p));
    const w = h & ((1n << BigInt(64 - p)) - 1n);
    const rank = 1 + clz64(w);
    if (this.reg[j] < rank) this.reg[j] = rank;
  }
  estimate() {
    // bias-corrected estimator placeholder
  }
}
```

