## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of streaming data structures and complexity trade-offs for maintaining order statistics under batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Running median via two heaps
- Per-element insert cost and batch amortization
- Memory growth with total elements

## Code Example
```javascript
class MedianMaintainer {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();
    this.high = new MinHeap();
  }
  add(x) {
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || x <= this.low.peek()) this.low.push(x);
    else this.high.push(x);
    // rebalance
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) this.low.push(this.high.pop());
  }
  median() {
    if (this.low.size() === this.high.size()) return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
    return this.low.peek();
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does varying batch size B affect latency and memory?
- How would you extend to handle sliding windows or duplicates efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic triangle counting with tight amortized analysis, batching, and memory trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic graphs, incremental triangles
- Degree-based edge orientation, forward neighbor sets
- Amortized analysis, batch processing, space efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
// high-level sketch
const Nplus = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> new Set());
const deg = new Int32Array(n);
let triangles = 0;
function addEdge(u,v){
  // decide orientation
  if (deg[u] > deg[v] || (deg[u]===deg[v] && u>v)) [u,v] = [v,u];
  // count new triangles
  let t = 0;
  for (let w of Nplus[u]) if (Nplus[v].has(w)) t++;
  triangles += t;
  Nplus[u].add(v);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
  // potentially rebalance orientation lazily
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does the method adapt if deletions are allowed?
- What are practical bottlenecks on real graphs (power-law networks)?
